NAME
       parted - create, delete and change disk partitions

SYNOPSIS
       parted [OPTIONS] [DEVICE [COMMAND [PARAMETERS]...]...]

DESCRIPTION
       parted (GNU Parted) edits the partition table of a disk. It works with
       both kinds of table used on PCs: msdos (MBR, up to four primary
       partitions, or three plus an extended one holding logical partitions,
       disks up to 2 TiB) and gpt (up to 128 partitions, very large disks).

       Started with only a DEVICE, parted shows its own "(parted)" prompt
       and reads commands one at a time until quit. With commands after the
       DEVICE it runs them and exits, which suits scripts and quick changes.

       Unlike fdisk and gdisk, parted writes each change to the disk at once:
       there is no separate "write" step and no undo. Check the device name
       before you press Enter.

       parted tells the kernel about the new table itself; when a disk is in
       use, run partprobe or udevadm settle afterwards. Creating a partition
       does not create a file system on it: use mkfs, mkswap or pvcreate
       next. The file system type given to mkpart only sets the partition's
       type code (for example linux-swap); it formats nothing.

OPTIONS
       -l, --list
              List the partition tables of all disks.

       -s, --script
              Never ask questions (for scripts). Commands that would need an
              answer fail instead; mklabel on a disk that already has a table
              goes ahead without asking.

       -m, --machine
              Print in a form that is easy for programs to read.

       -a, --align none|minimal|optimal
              How new partitions are aligned. The default, optimal, starts
              them on 1 MiB boundaries.

COMMANDS
       print [free]
              Show the table: model, size, table type and each partition.
              "print free" also shows the free space.

       mklabel TYPE
              Write a new, empty partition table of TYPE msdos or gpt. Every
              partition on the disk is lost.

       mkpart [PART-TYPE | NAME] [FS-TYPE] START END
              Make a partition. On msdos disks PART-TYPE is primary,
              extended or logical. On gpt disks the first word is the
              partition's name. FS-TYPE is a hint such as xfs, ext4,
              linux-swap or fat32. START and END are positions on the disk,
              with a unit: 1MiB, 1025MiB, 5GiB, or a percentage such as 100%.

       rm NUMBER
              Delete a partition. It must not be mounted or in use.

       set NUMBER FLAG on|off
              Set a flag on a partition: lvm, swap, raid, boot, esp,
              bios_grub.

       name NUMBER NAME
              Name a gpt partition.

       resizepart NUMBER END
              Move the end of a partition (to make it bigger or smaller). The
              file system inside is not resized.

       unit UNIT
              The unit for printing and for positions given without one: s
              (sectors), B, KiB, MiB, GiB, MB, GB, % or compact.

       help [COMMAND], quit, version

       Units: MiB and GiB are powers of 1024, MB and GB powers of 1000. Use
       MiB and GiB to get the sizes the exam asks for; a partition from 1MiB
       to 1025MiB is exactly 1 GiB.

EXAMPLES
       Look at a disk:

              # parted /dev/vdb print

       Give /dev/vdb a GPT table and a 1 GiB partition for XFS:

              # parted /dev/vdb mklabel gpt
              # parted /dev/vdb mkpart data xfs 1MiB 1025MiB

       An MBR disk with a 512 MiB swap partition and an LVM partition in
       the rest of the disk:

              # parted -s /dev/vdc mklabel msdos
              # parted -s /dev/vdc mkpart primary linux-swap 1MiB 513MiB
              # parted -s /dev/vdc mkpart primary 513MiB 100%
              # parted -s /dev/vdc set 2 lvm on

       Interactive use:

              # parted /dev/vdb
              (parted) print free
              (parted) mkpart backup ext4 1025MiB 2049MiB
              (parted) quit

       Delete partition 2:

              # parted /dev/vdb rm 2

NOTES
       Partitions made by parted end one sector before the END you give, so
       consecutive partitions can share a boundary value (1MiB 513MiB, then
       513MiB 1025MiB). After changing partitions that are listed in
       /etc/fstab, check the file (parted reminds you of this).

SEE ALSO
       fdisk(8), gdisk(8), sgdisk(8), partprobe(8), lsblk(8), mkfs(8),
       mkswap(8), pvcreate(8)
